<p class="p12">&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>* Tenno Sho (Autumn) (G1)</strong></p>
<p>&ldquo;The Emperor&rsquo;s Cup&rdquo; was first held in 1905 to promote the horse racing sport through annual events that gave the winner the highest honor of being awarded the Imperial prize. Then the Tenno Sho (Autumn) was established in 1937 together with its counterpart, Tenno Sho (Spring), under the name &ldquo;Teishitsu Goshoten Kyoso&rdquo; and held biannually in both the Eastern and Western parts of Japan. The race was officially renamed the &ldquo;Tenno Sho&rdquo; in 1947. Both the spring and autumn races, which secured their status as the most prestigious events for older horses, were run over 3,200 meters in the early years. Later, the autumn version was shortened to 2,000 meters in 1984 so that the spring version could determine the best stayer, while the Tenno Sho (Autumn) would be the main target for middle-distance champions. This also gives the three-year-olds, who find the third leg of the Triple Crown, the Kikuka Sho (Japanese St. Leger, G1, 3,000m), to be beyond their suitability, an alternative G1 as the ultimate goal for their autumn campaign. The Tenno Sho (Autumn) entered a new chapter in its long history when opening its doors to foreign contenders as an international race in 2005.<br>
This year, defending champion <strong>Masquerade Ball</strong> came into the race aiming to bounce back from a dismal seventh in the King George VI And Queen Elizabeth Stakes (G1, 2,390m, Jul.25). Four-time G1 winner <strong>Croix du Nord</strong> and 2024 Tokyo Yushun (Japanese Derby, G1, 2,400m) victor <strong>Danon Decile</strong> came off a close second and a third, respectively, in the Takarazuka Kinen (G1, 2,200m, Jun.14). <strong>Seiun Hades</strong> won his third graded title in the Mainichi Okan (G2, 1,800m, Oct.4), one of the main prep races, with 2024 Tenno Sho (Autumn) third-place finisher <strong>Ho O Biscuits</strong> behind him in second and the Satsuki Sho (Japanese 2000 Guineas, G1, 2,000m) runner-up <strong>Realize Sirius</strong> in seventh. The field also included three-time G1 champion mare <strong>Regaleira </strong>and last year&rsquo;s Arima Kinen (G1, 2,500m) runner-up <strong>Cosmo Kuranda</strong>, who finished fourth and 11th, respectively, in the All Comers (G2, 2,200m, Sep.20). <strong>Shake Your Heart</strong> and <strong>Giovanni</strong>, respective winners of the Sapporo Kinen (G2, 2,000m, Aug.16) and the Challenge Cup (G3, 2,000m, Sep.12) were also among the lineup.</p></td>
